from pathlib import Path
import json,re,datetime
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
CONTEXT=ROOT.parent/'context'
notes=[]
def add(id,title,kind,loc,quote,terms,context,ledger,body):
 notes.append(dict(id=id,title=title,kind=kind,location=loc,quote=quote,terms=terms,context=context,ledger=ledger,body=body))
# Witness locations always refer to frozen individual-page TeX lines.
add('A01','Comparing characteristic-p and characteristic-zero fibres','unproved claim','15/195:L9–19','Ils ne sont en général pas isomorphes; sans doute; Vérifier',['Witt','Frobenius','cristall','caractéristique'],'Adds to CT54/003,005,007 (de Rham and Cartier-duality comparisons); no matching general mixed-characteristic assertion','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]; MPL_14_002-044 M28a [first-pass lead, not a result used here]',r'The proposed non-isomorphism compares integral or rational realizations over different coefficient rings, so an identification of coefficient rings and the structures to be preserved must precede either a proof or a counterexample. The finite-residue-field qualification is partly deleted and uncertain, and the cancelled “Vérifier” cannot be promoted to a verified comparison. Oda (1969, Corollary 5.11) supplies a precise degree-one comparison in characteristic p, not the unrestricted mixed-characteristic identification conjectured here.')
add('A02','Two filtrations with different origins','reading question','15/195:L21–23','ne provient pas d’une filtration du motif',['filtration','Hodge','extension canonique'],'Adds to CT54/003,005 and CN D01–D02; their Hodge exact sequence is a concrete instance, not a definition of this manuscript’s second filtration','MPL_14_002-044 M01, M29a [first-pass leads]',r'The margin distinguishes a filtration intrinsic to the motive from the Hodge filtration of its de Rham realization. Its displayed associated-graded description contains the uncertain coefficient reading \(\mathbf Q_l\), which must remain a reading question rather than become a retrospectively corrected de Rham formula. The earlier effectivity and mixed-extension leads do not fix either convention on this sheet.')
add('A03','Semilinear descent does not supply monodromy','unpursued implication','15/197:L2;15/201:L16','Il n’y a plus en général d’opération de π₁(X)',['descente','foncteur','Hodge-DeRham'],'Adds to CT54/003’s de Rham realization; differs from CT16/019–023’s étale cohomological setting','MPL_14_002-044 M01 [first-pass lead]; WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]',r'The pages repeatedly separate automorphisms of the coefficient field acting semilinearly from a fundamental-group action on a fibre. A comparison argument must therefore specify its base point, scalar extension and descent datum before transporting an action. This distinction limits any attempt to read the later discrete motivic group as an already-defined topological or étale fundamental group.')
add('A04','The cohomological degree of the fibre-functor obstruction','reading question','15/197:L4–6','une classe de coh dans H^{2|1}',['torseur','cohomologie','foncteur'],'Adds to CT54/003’s extension-class discussion; no matching fibre-functor obstruction formula in CT','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]; MPL_7_061-072+8_001-078 Scan 061 (§66) [first-pass lead]',r'The frozen exponent is explicitly uncertain between 2 and 1. After fixing one fibre functor, isomorphism classes of its forms are ordinarily classified by nonabelian \(H^1\) of its tensor-automorphism group, whereas a gerbe-neutralization obstruction is a different, degree-two question (Deligne & Milne, 1982, §§2–3). This gives a contextual reason to revisit the ink in a future edition, not permission to change the frozen exponent or to assert which obstruction was intended.')
add('A05','Absence of a canonical rational comparison','unproved claim','15/197:L4;15/199:L2','il n’y a pas d’isom canonique prévisible',['Tate','Hodge','corps'],'Adds to CT54/003,007 and Oda1969’s characteristic-p comparison; differs in base field and coefficient ring','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]; MPL_7_061-072+8_001-078 Scan 061 (§66) [first-pass lead]',r'The text distinguishes non-canonicity from the stronger possibility that no isomorphism exists over \(\mathbf Q_l\). A later comparison after adjoining a period ring does not establish an isomorphism over that original coefficient field, and an integral lattice is additional data. The marginal example involving weight and level zero suggests testing the assertion first on Artin realizations, with their descent data specified.')
add('A06','Archimedean places and integral Betti comparison','unpursued implication','15/199:L4–25;15/201:L2–14','isom canonique “involutif”',['Betti','dualité','Hodge'],'Adds to CT54/003,005’s de Rham duality by specifying an integral Betti lattice and conjugation','LM7 C.1 [Known, return’s claim]; MPL_14_002-044 M24a [first-pass lead]',r'The page identifies the Betti lattice after extension to \(\mathbf Z_l\) or \(\mathbf C\), and then explains why a conjugate complex point gives an involutively related lattice. That comparison does not say that the lattice alone recovers the archimedean place. The distinction becomes explicit in the retraction on scans 207 and 225.')
add('A07','The two semilinear conjugations','self-flagged','15/203:L9–17','τ = τ′τ″ = τ″τ′; à vérifier',['Hodge','transposée','dualité'],'Adds to CT54/003,005; no two-conjugation identity located in CT','MPL_14_002-044 A36 [first-pass lead]; WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]',r'The author marks the commuting-product identity itself for verification. A proof must compare conjugation induced by the Betti real form with conjugation induced by real de Rham descent under the chosen comparison isomorphism. Two semilinear involutions have a linear product, but their commutativity does not follow merely from semilinearity.')
add('A08','Real Frobenius compatibility','self-flagged','15/203:L19–24;15/205:L2','frobenius en la place réelle; à vérifier',['Frobenius','Tate','dualité'],'Adds to CT54/007’s Frobenius–Verschiebung diagram; differs by using archimedean conjugation rather than characteristic-p Frobenius','MPL_14_002-044 B04 [first-pass lead]; LM6 A.50 [Proved, return’s claim]',r'The second verification mark concerns the identification of the real involution with the corresponding action on an l-adic fibre. The later continuation asserts the resulting integral action of \(f_\infty\), but supplies no separate compatibility proof. Neither the common word “Frobenius” nor the normalizer result in the register identifies this archimedean operator with a finite-field Frobenius.')
add('A09','Hodge theory as a motivic-level conjecture','conjecture','15/205:L4–13','La partie de niveau; le dernier sous-espace … est motivique',['Hodge','index','algébrique'],'Adds to CT16/044,049 and CT44/004,008–009; these discuss algebraicity and positivity, not this intersection formula','MPL_14_002-044 M01, M29a [first-pass leads]; WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]',r'The formula proposes that an intersection involving the rational Betti realization, a Hodge-filtration term and its \(f_\infty\)-translate comes from a submotive, although the individual Hodge terms do not. The uncertain level index and the meaning of “niveau” must be fixed before identifying this with a particular generalized Hodge conjecture. Grothendieck (1969b, §1) explicitly distinguishes the rational algebraic-class conjecture from a stronger filtration statement requiring correction; that printed discussion is relevant without establishing an exact equivalence with this draft.')
add('A10','The rational-subspace test for the level formula','own idea','15/205:L8–13','provient d’un sous-motif',['Hodge','filtration','motives'],'Adds a test to CT16/049’s subobject/splitting programme and CN S14–S16','MPL_14_002-044 M29a [first-pass lead]; WG2 §2.3 #7 [unchecked plan]',r'Before using the displayed intersection as a motivic subobject, test that its complexification is stable under the Hodge decomposition, not merely under conjugation. A submotive would have that stability, while an arbitrary rational subspace lying in a band of Hodge types need not have it. Confidence: high for this necessary-condition test, because functoriality of a Hodge realization forces subobjects to be Hodge substructures; no geometric counterexample to the manuscript’s undefined level convention is asserted.')
add('A11','Retraction of separation by bare integral or real fibres','withdrawn in the same text','15/207:L2–4;15/219:L24–35,50–53;15/221:L3–19;15/225:L3','Je pense; faux, cf groupoïde fondamental sur Z; très loin d’être caractérisée',['groupoïde','foncteur','Betti'],'Adds a documented internal retraction; CT contains no parallel archimedean-place separation claim','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]; MPL_14_002-044 §3 [first-pass lead]',r'The claim that distinct places always yield non-isomorphic integral or real fibre functors is explicitly marked “faux” on scan 207. Scans 219–221 supply finite-type and countable-field isomorphism arguments, and scan 225 restates the failure of place recovery from the bare \(\mathbf Z\)-module functor. The later number-field separation question is narrower and remains distinct from this withdrawn unrestricted assertion.')
add('A12','Real neutralization in positive characteristic','conjecture','15/207:L8–10','T_α(R)=∅ pour α grand; Vérifier … ext. tot. réelle ???',['caractéristique','dualité','Tate'],'Adds to CT54/005,007’s positive-characteristic realization problems; no matching real-neutralization assertion found','MPL_7_061-072+8_001-078 Scan 061 (§66) [first-pass lead]; WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]',r'The expectation is the nonexistence of real-valued fibre functors for sufficiently large finite pieces of the category, with a separate question about totally real extensions. It requires an actual obstruction to real neutralization, not merely the absence of a geometric complex-conjugation operation in characteristic p. The sheet gives neither the required finite subcategory nor an obstruction class.')
add('A13','A gerbe of points and individual path torsors','reading question','15/207:L16;15/209:L2','catégorie fibrée … fpqc; c’est un torseur; classes de chemins',['torseur','foncteur','extension universelle'],'Adds to CT54/003’s universal-extension language; no equivalent fibre-functor gerbe construction in CT','MPL_7_061-072+8_001-078 Scan 061 (§66) [first-pass lead]; WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]',r'The category of fibre functors is naturally a gerbe, whereas the isomorphisms between two chosen functors form a torsor under a tensor-automorphism group when locally nonempty (Deligne & Milne, 1982, §3). The manuscript moves from “catégorie fibrée” to “torseur” without spelling out that distinction. A reconstruction should keep its groupoid of points and its individual path objects separate.')
add('A14','Twisting a fixed motivic point','unproved claim','15/209:L2','tous les autres pts s’en déduisent par des fibrés principaux',['fibré','extension','foncteur'],'Adds to CT54/003’s extension classification; the torsor classification here is a different construction','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]; MPL_7_061-072+8_001-078 Scan 061 (§66) [first-pass lead]',r'The assertion is a descent statement relative to one fibre functor and its affine tensor-automorphism group. Its integral version requires exactness, local freeness and an appropriate reconstruction theorem over the chosen ring; the neutral Tannakian theorem over a field cannot by itself justify every ring-valued case. This is the structural prerequisite for the later proposed classification of places by torsors.')
add('A15','The proposed bijection with enriched transcendental points','conjecture','15/209:L6–12;15/211:L18–20','Il semble … biunivoquement; structure supplémentaire',['Hodge','foncteur','motives'],'Adds to CT16/049’s category-level programme; no such bijection in CT','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]; MPL_14_002-044 §3 [first-pass lead]',r'The bracketed “structure supplémentaire” is decisive: the proposed bijection cannot be read as the bare-lattice statement already marked false. Scans 225–227 later ask which Hodge-valued tensor functors are geometric. The unspecified enrichment is therefore part of the open question, not a completed classification.')
add('A16','Crystalline points as a reconstruction problem','self-flagged','15/211:L3–15,33–36','Questions; À revoir; avec structure supplémentaire (F,V)',['Frobenius','Verschiebung','Dieudonné','Hodge'],'Adds to CT54/005,007; restates the need to retain F,V while enlarging the problem from abelian degree one to motives','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]; MPL_14_002-044 M24a [first-pass lead]',r'The page is headed “Questions” and marked “À revoir”, so the arrows from geometric points to filtered or crystalline motivic points are proposals. Oda’s degree-one result identifies the relevant Dieudonné module and Hodge sequence for an abelian variety over a perfect field, but does not classify arbitrary motives by a Witt-valued point with two operators (Oda, 1969, Corollary 5.11). The base field’s perfection and the integral versus isogeny setting must remain explicit.')
add('A17','Filtered points in characteristic zero','conjecture','15/211:L9–10;15/227:L3','correspondent aux pts motiviques dans h, avec structure filtrée',['filtration','Hodge','extension'],'Adds to CT54/003’s filtered de Rham sequence; no essential-image criterion there','MPL_14_002-044 M01, M29a [first-pass leads]; WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]',r'The proposed correspondence needs an essential-image condition, since a filtered fibre functor alone does not include all geometric, rational and positivity constraints. The later explicit essential-image question on scan 227 confirms that this is not a theorem proved by the tabular arrows. A modern comparison should specify whether the target includes Betti data, polarization, Griffiths transversality or an admissibility condition.')
add('A18','A discrete automorphism group is not automatically π₁ top','reading question','15/213:L3–8,22–30','Groupe fondamental motivique discret; Comparaison avec π₁',['groupe','foncteur','motives'],'Adds to CT16/049; differs from CT16/047, where π₁ denotes a cohomological Künneth projector, not a fundamental group','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]; MPL_14_002-044 §3 [first-pass lead]',r'The definition is \(\operatorname{Aut}^{\otimes}(\xi)=G_\xi(\mathbf Z)\), followed by a question of comparison with the topological fundamental group. A monodromy homomorphism into this automorphism group need not be injective or surjective. In particular, the \(\pi_1\) occurring in the context’s discussion of algebraic projectors must not be used as evidence for a fundamental-group identification.')
add('A19','Arithmetic finiteness needs a specified orbit problem','unproved claim','15/213:L10–14','En vertu de Borel-Harish; nb fini',['Borel','Harish','fini','semi-simple'],'Adds to CT16/049’s finite-dimensional semisimplicity discussion; no Borel–Harish-Chandra application found in CT','WG2 §2.3 #7, WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plans]',r'The intended finiteness must concern isomorphism classes, since a category of fibre functors is not literally a finite set of its presentations. To apply arithmetic reduction theory one must specify the algebraic group, integral model, tensors or orbit, and the requisite finiteness hypotheses; finite covolume alone does not count these forms (Borel & Harish-Chandra, 1962). The question whether the resulting discrete groups are mutually isomorphic is a further claim, not a consequence of the asserted finiteness.')
add('A20','Full faithfulness from Hodge and Tate','unproved claim','15/213:L32–37;15/215:L3–9','est … fidèle; conjonction des conj. de Hodge et de Tate',['Hodge','Tate','motives','algébrique'],'Adds to CT16/049’s semisimple-motives yoga and CT44/008’s realization proposals','MPL_14_087-132 M09 [first-pass lead]; WG2 §2.3 #7, WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plans]',r'The claimed fully faithful functor includes both monodromy and Hodge structure on a fibre, and the continuation explicitly makes it conditional on Hodge and finite-field Tate conjectures, at least for normal X. A proof would need a spreading or specialization argument and a precise category of motives over X, neither supplied here. Tate’s theorem for homomorphisms of abelian varieties over finite fields is a narrower input, not this global statement (Tate, 1966).')
add('A21','The essential image remains open','conjecture','15/215:L3–5','Caractériser l’image ??',['Hodge','motives','foncteur'],'Adds to CT16/049’s categorical expectation; no essential-image characterization found','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]; MPL_14_002-044 §3 [first-pass lead]',r'Full faithfulness, even if granted, does not identify which monodromy representations with Hodge data are realizations of motives. The double question mark asks precisely for this missing image condition. Scans 217 and 227 suggest moduli of polarized motives as an approach without resolving it.')
add('A22','Serre’s conjugate-variety example is a test, not the ring example','self-flagged','15/215:L11–12;15/223:L2','Analyser l’exemple de Serre de π₁ non isomorphes',['Serre','simply connected','cohomologie'],'Adds to CT16/047’s different Serre objection to a hypersurface reduction; the two uses of Serre concern different claims','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]',r'The author asks to analyze conjugate varieties with non-isomorphic topological fundamental groups. That property does not, on its own, imply that their integral cohomology rings differ, which is the stronger criterion sought on scan 223. Charles (2009, introduction and Theorem 1) explicitly separates the earlier fundamental-group examples from the later cohomology-algebra construction.')
add('A23','Real descent and generation by real Frobenius','unproved claim','15/215:L14–26','avec la comp. conn. … engendre G; pleinement fidèle',['polarisation','Hodge','dualité'],'Adds to CT44/004,008–009’s positivity programme; no corresponding real-descent generation assertion','MPL_14_002-044 A36 [first-pass lead]; WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]',r'The page claims that real Frobenius together with the connected component generates the motivic group, and that Hodge structure plus the indicated realification is fully faithful. These depend on identifying the component quotient and the relevant descent category, in addition to the Hodge assertion. The correction from “géom. connexe” to “irred (pas néc. géom irred)” must be retained as an explicit change of hypothesis, not silently strengthened back.')
add('A24','Recovering a variation from one fibre','conjecture','15/215:L35–37;15/217:L3–8','étudier comment varie la structure de Hodge',['Hodge','modules','polarisation'],'Adds to CT44/002,004’s polarized abelian-variety programme and CT47/003’s relative setup','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]; MPL_14_002-044 M24a [first-pass lead]',r'The question concerns how a Hodge structure varies when a complex point moves, given data at a base point. Monodromy and one fibre do not by themselves specify a general period map, so the next page’s rigidity assertion requires hypotheses on the family and its target moduli space. A proof should identify the exact rigidity mechanism rather than replace it by a general statement about local systems.')
add('A25','Rigidity of a map to a motivic moduli space','unproved claim','15/217:L3–8','déterminé … en un point, plus l’application π₁',['modules','motifs','polarisation'],'Adds to CT47/003’s abelian-scheme setting and CT44/004’s polarization; no stated map-rigidity theorem in CT','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]; MPL_14_002-044 §3 [first-pass lead]',r'The assertion fixes both the value of a map and its induced fundamental-group map. It needs a well-defined rigidified moduli functor and hypotheses excluding nonconstant deformations with unchanged discrete data. The bare assertion is substantially broader than a fully faithful comparison for one motive.')
add('A26','Characterizing varieties by motivic rigidity','conjecture','15/217:L10–16','Peut-être … interprétées comme … variétés modulaires',['modules','motives','polarisation'],'Adds to CT16/049’s categorical yoga; no moduli characterization in CT','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]',r'The author asks whether varieties possessing the stated rigidity property can all be interpreted as moduli spaces of certain motifs. This is an inverse construction problem, distinct from proving rigidity for a known moduli space. Its quantifier “toutes” and the unresolved class of permitted motifs should be recorded before seeking special cases.')
add('A27','Motivically pointed schemes','unpursued implication','15/217:L20–27','Introduire la notion de schéma … pointé',['foncteur','motives','Sch'],'Adds to CT16/049’s category programme and CT47/003’s base-scheme functoriality','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]',r'The proposed base point is a fibre functor valued in an arbitrary ring h, giving a tensor-automorphism group and its h-points. To define the induced group homomorphism, the morphism of pointed schemes must include a compatible identification of fibre functors. Forgetting that identification leaves an inner-conjugacy ambiguity, which matters when comparing the construction with anabelian Hom statements.')
add('A28','Finite tensor subcategories versus one global isomorphism','conjecture','15/219:L5–10','pour tte sous-catégorie … de t.f.',['foncteur','inverse','limite'],'Adds to CT16/049’s finite-dimensional correspondence arguments; no compatible-system assertion in CT','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]',r'The sheet explicitly asks two different questions: isomorphism of the full fibre functors, and separate isomorphisms on every finitely generated tensor subcategory. Passing from the latter to the former requires compatible choices in an inverse system of isomorphism torsors. The distinction explains why the subsequent countability argument is mathematically substantive.')
add('A29','Spreading out the finite-subcategory comparison','unproved claim','15/219:L24–35,55–56','grâce au groupoïde fondamental transcendant',['connexe','type fini','spécialisation'],'Adds a Betti-path method to CT16/045–046’s specialization arguments; differs from their algebraic-cycle conclusions','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]',r'When the places agree on the algebraic part of the field, the sketch spreads X and a finite tensor subcategory to a finite-type model and appeals to a connected complex groupoid. A complete proof must show that the chosen motifs, their tensor relations and the required integral comparison all descend to that model. Normality and geometric connectedness are written ingredients and should not disappear in a reformulation.')
add('A30','The countable inverse limit of path sets','unproved claim','15/221:L3–17','les morphismes de transition … sont surjectifs; on gagne',['limite','connexe','lisse'],'Adds to CT16/045–046’s reduction techniques; no matching countable inverse-path argument in CT','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]',r'The sketch reduces to a sequence of smooth models with geometrically connected fibres and claims surjectivity on path classes. Nonempty sets with surjective maps in a countable inverse system admit a compatible sequence by successive choices, but obtaining that particular system of models and those surjections remains the geometric step. The uncertain superscript on π is retained and cannot be used to decide whether the intended groupoid is topological or completed.')
add('A31','The uncountable case is unfinished','self-flagged','15/221:L19','Si k pas dénombrable,',['limite','type fini','connexe'],'Adds an explicit boundary to CT16/045–046’s finite reduction pattern; no uncountable construction located','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]',r'The phrase ends without an argument. It marks the scope limit of the preceding countable construction rather than proving either failure or success for uncountable fields. No compactness or inverse-limit theorem is supplied to remove that limit.')
add('A32','Separation on the algebraic part of the field','conjecture','15/221:L21–29','je pense qu’il existe α',['Serre','motives','corps'],'Adds to CT16/049’s finite motivic pieces; narrows the withdrawn unrestricted assertion of15/207','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]',r'For places differing on the algebraic part E, the author expects a finitely generated tensor subcategory on which the integral fibre functors differ. The reduction to a number field is stated, but the frozen \(\operatorname{Spec}(E|k)\) and surrounding field notation remain uncertain. An existence example of one conjugate pair does not prove this separation for every pair.')
add('A33','A multiplicative cohomology witness','unpursued implication','15/223:L2–3','non isomorphes comme anneaux; remplacer Z par R??',['Serre','cohomologie','anneau'],'Adds to CT16/015–018’s correspondence-ring emphasis; no conjugate cohomology-ring example in CT','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]; MPL_14_087-132 M01 [first-pass lead]',r'A tensor isomorphism of fibre functors must preserve the multiplication morphism of a cohomology algebra object, so a non-isomorphic graded ring is a suitable witness even when the underlying groups agree. Charles (2009, Theorems 1–2) constructs smooth projective conjugates over a number field with non-isomorphic real cohomology algebras, answering the existential real-coefficient version of the margin’s question. This does not establish universal separation of prescribed places or identify those examples with the ones Serre was asked to provide.')
add('A34','Injectivity of the place-to-torsor map','conjecture','15/223:L6–9','si M est pris assez grand',['fibré','motives','fini'],'Adds to CT16/049’s finite-category programme; no place-to-torsor injection in CT','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]',r'The conjecture asserts that a sufficiently large motive separates archimedean places through the isomorphism classes of associated integral torsors. The qualification on M is essential: a small realization can forget places. One must also decide whether a single M is meant to separate all places at once and specify the integral group scheme attached to it.')
add('A35','Surjectivity onto all torsors','conjecture','15/223:L10–11','∀ fibré principal homogène P … provient d’une place',['torseur','fibré','polarisation'],'Adds to CT16/049’s reconstruction programme; no arithmetic torsor-surjectivity theorem in CT','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]',r'This is stronger than injectivity and is immediately qualified by the marginal Riemann–Hodge compatibility warning. The appropriate target may be torsors equipped with a complex comparison satisfying positivity and Hodge constraints, rather than every unadorned torsor under the integral group. The manuscript asks the question but does not define that restricted target.')
add('A36','Different torsors need not imply non-isomorphic abstract groups','own idea','15/223:L14–17','ces groupes à priori ne sont pas isomorphes',['groupe','semi-simple','isomorphisme'],'Adds a logical test to CT16/049’s group/category discussion; no abstract-group classification in CT','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]; LM1 A.8 [Refuted, return’s claim]',r'Separate the torsor-class comparison from the abstract isomorphism problem for the groups of integral points of the twisted automorphism groups. The first does not formally settle the second, since forgetting a group scheme, tensors or its action loses information. Confidence: high for this distinction, because an isomorphism of abstract groups is a weaker structure than an isomorphism of the specified tensor torsors; no specific counterexample to the manuscript’s arithmetic setting is claimed.')
add('A37','Does Hodge enrichment recover the place?','conjecture','15/225:L3,9,16','que se passe-t-il … structures de Hodge ?',['Hodge','filtration','motives'],'Adds to CT54/003,005’s Hodge data and CT16/049’s motive category; no geometric essential-image theorem in CT','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]; MPL_14_002-044 §3 [first-pass lead]',r'The question deliberately restores the Hodge structure after the failure of the bare integral functor. The margin warns that a complex place must be specified appropriately for that structure, so quotienting by conjugation and forgetting it are not interchangeable operations. The deleted reduction through the projective line is a discarded route, not a proof of reconstruction.')
add('A38','The elliptic j-family as a test case','unproved claim','15/225:L16','Il semble qu’on le verrait facilement; invariant … j',['courbe','Hodge','VA'],'Adds a proposed family to CT54/003,005 and CT44/008’s abelian realizations','MPL_14_002-044 M24a [first-pass lead]; WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]',r'The suggested test uses an elliptic family over a punctured projective line whose j-invariant is the parameter. Making it precise requires distinguishing the j-line from a fine modular curve with level structure, and checking the omitted special values and existence of a universal family. An integral polarized Hodge structure can distinguish much more than its rational isogeny class, so the coefficient and polarization choices are part of the test.')
add('A39','Reconstructing maps from tensor functors','conjecture','15/227:L2–3','dans quelle mesure … un foncteur … peut-il être pris arbitrairement ?!?',['foncteur','motives','Hodge'],'Adds to CT16/049’s categorical yoga; no anabelian Hom assertion in CT','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]; MPL_14_002-044 §3; MPL_14_087-132 §81 [first-pass leads]',r'The page asks both faithfulness of the passage from scheme maps to motivic tensor functors and characterization of its image. These are distinct from the Tate comparison of linear Hom spaces and from an anabelian theorem for specified hyperbolic curves. The marginal appeal to Hodge is a proposed input, not a proof covering arbitrary preschemes.')
add('A40','The proposed moduli immersion criterion','unproved claim','15/227:L5','Y … Modules est une immersion; anabélien',['modules','polarisation','motives'],'Adds to CT44/004,008’s polarization programme; no moduli-immersion criterion in CT','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]; MPL_14_002-044 §3 [first-pass lead]',r'The proposed implication would use a global polarized motive on Y and an immersive map to a moduli space to recover maps into Y. The uncertain word “anabélien”, the kind of immersion and the class of source schemes all need clarification before this can be treated as an anabelian statement. The last sentence about spectra of fields is an expectation without a proof on this sheet.')
# Reused sheets: their editorial voice is not automatically the author of the facing notes.
add('R01','Reused Lie-theory text is not motivic evidence','reading question','15/196:L4–18;15/200:L18–22;15/206:L6–20','ALGÈBRES de LIE SEMI-SIMPLES; représentations de sl₂',['sl_2','Lefschetz','racines'],'Differs from CT16/006–018’s Lefschetz operators; CT54/008 provides a parallel instance of reused root-system text','WG2 §2.4 #3 [unchecked plan]; MPL_14_087-132 M04 [first-pass lead]',r'These are editorial/typescript sheets from another text, with Tribu and rédaction numbers. Their occurrence of \(\mathfrak{sl}_2\), Cartan and roots is not evidence that the handwritten motivic Galois group contains the Lefschetz action. The heading and reuse classification must remain attached to any mathematical quotation from these leaves.')
add('R02','Finite-dimensional hypotheses in the representation text','unproved claim','15/198:L12–16','Ajouter “de dim. finie”; pas si “facile”',['dimension','semi-simple','Lefschetz'],'Differs from CT16/006–018’s graded Lefschetz algebra; supplies an independent editorial restriction','WG2 §2.3 #2 [unchecked plan, analogy only; no topical settled entry]',r'The reused editorial sheet repeatedly adds finite-dimensionality and replaces an allegedly easy argument by a Casimir calculation. These changes identify a missing hypothesis and a deferred proof in the source being revised. They do not supply a proof of semisimplicity for a motivic category.')
add('R03','An explicitly unchecked rank formula','self-flagged','15/202:L12','Vérifier la formule du bas de la p. 9',['formule','vérifier','racines'],'Differs from CT54/008’s root-system fragment; the referenced p.9 is not supplied','WG2 §1.1 #3 [unchecked organizational plan; no mathematical result ID]',r'The editorial instruction explicitly requests verification of a formula on a different page. That target page is absent, so the formula cannot be reconstructed from the instruction. This is a genuine unverified-step entry even though it belongs to reused typescript.')
add('R04','Characteristic-two and positive-characteristic corrections','withdrawn in the same text','15/202:L12–18','ne sont vraies qu’en caract. ≠2; fausse en caract. p',['caract','nilpotent','racines'],'Differs from CT16/014’s separate integral Lefschetz issue; no identical correction in CT','WG2 §2.3 #3 [unchecked plan, different integral problem]',r'The sheet withdraws a characteristic-free formulation, citing nilpotence of \(\mathfrak{sl}_2\) in characteristic 2, then orders two further false positive-characteristic assertions removed or replaced. Their original statements lie on unsupplied pages 10, 15 and the indicated final remark, so only the corrections themselves are witnessed here. All corrections are on scan 202; no later motivic theorem is being retracted.')
add('R05','Deleted source propositions and a corrected summation','withdrawn in the same text','15/204:L10–18','c’est faux; la sommation est incorrecte',['sommation','formule','racines'],'Differs from CT54/008’s root-system page; referenced source pp.1–5 are absent','WG2 §1.1 #3 [unchecked organizational plan; no topical result ID]',r'The editorial text rejects specified propositions and instructs that a summation run over half the roots. Without the referenced earlier pages, one cannot reconstruct the original false statements or determine all hypotheses of the replacement. The record therefore preserves the withdrawal and correction without assigning them to the motivic discussion.')
add('R06','Root-definition symbols retained literally','reading question','15/208:L4;15/210:L12','[a,x]=(a).x; [h,x]=α(x)',['racine','root','système'],'Restates the root-system subject of CT54/008 but differs in the displayed definitions','WG2 §1.1 #3 [unchecked organizational plan; no topical result ID]',r'The frozen typescript formulas do not match the expected typed expression \([h,x]=\alpha(h)x\): one lacks a visible α before \((a)\), and the other has \(\alpha(x)\). Since x lies in the Lie algebra rather than necessarily in the Cartan domain of α, this is a mathematical type check and a future source-collation question. No missing symbol is supplied to the transcription.')
add('R07','The domain condition for local associativity','reading question','15/216:L24–34','m(x,m(y,z)) appartiennent à Ω',['associativité','formel','noyau'],'Differs from CT16/025’s explicitly boxed associativity conditions; both show why the domain must be specified','WG2 §1.1 #3 [unchecked organizational plan; no topical result ID]',r'The fourth listed member of Ω is a product value in G, although Ω was defined as a subset of \(G\times G\). The likely mathematical condition concerns the pair \((x,m(y,z))\), but this is an editorial interpretation of a type mismatch, not a corrected reading. The following inverse calculation also retains its written parenthesis structure.')
add('R08','Deferred extensions to group germs','unproved claim','15/214:L15–17,24–34','Nous laisserons au lecteur le soin d’étendre',['groupe','formel','extension'],'Adds a separate reuse example alongside CT16/033–037 and CT54/008; no matching group-germ proof in CT','WG2 §1.1 #3 [unchecked organizational plan; no topical result ID]',r'The typescript leaves three sets of extensions to the reader: propositions 3 and 3 bis, the notation and results of nos.12–16, and propositions 11–12 with corollaries. A completion would have to control the domains of partially defined multiplication and the loss from \(C^r\) to \(C^{r-1}\) on tangent objects. These are deferred proofs in the reused source, not new conjectures about motives.')
add('R09','A later unimodularity theorem is only announced','unproved claim','15/220:L42–43;15/218:L22–37','Nous verrons plus tard … semi-simples ou nilpotents',['semi-simple','nilpotent','groupe'],'Differs from CT16/049’s motivic semisimplicity; the shared adjective does not identify the assertions','WG2 §2.3 #7 [unchecked plan, subject distinction]',r'The reused Haar-measure text announces unimodularity of connected semisimple or nilpotent Lie groups. The determinant expression on scan 218 suggests the missing proof through the adjoint modular character, but the promised later argument is absent. Unimodularity is not centre-freeness or semisimplicity of a tensor category.')
add('R10','Formal maps and coalgebra compatibility','reading question','18/016:L7–23','applications diagonales; a_{0,0}≠0',['formel','Cartier','dualité'],'Differs from CT54/007’s finite-group-scheme/restricted-Lie comparison; the reused page concerns formal-map coalgebras','WG2 §1.1 #3 [unchecked organizational plan; no topical result ID]',r'The proposition characterizes formal maps by a condition on the degree-zero part and compatibility with the diagonal. Its proof switches from modules M and N to M and M′, and writes \(a_{0,0}\ne0\), so the coefficient-ring assumptions and the meaning of equality of degree-zero submodules should be recovered from the missing preceding source. These notational issues do not establish an identification with the crystalline operators on scan 5.')
add('R11','Bibliographic reuse and dating','reading question','18/003:L2–26','Page 6; Proposal to National Science Foundation; To Appear, Summer 1969',['To Appear','bibliography','Shatz'],'Restates the codicological problem of CT16/028’s reused bibliography and CN16:28:1; differs in source and listed work','WG2 §1.1 #3 [unchecked organizational plan; no topical result ID]',r'The printed bibliography contains dated and forthcoming approximation-theory items, but its author is not named on this sheet. Those dates can constrain the preparation of the reused source only after bibliographic identification; they do not by themselves date the handwritten Hodge or period sheets. Its printed page 6 and visible manuscript number 2 are separate pagination systems.')
